## 2. Dummy body is still 02 00 0b

Code section walk in emit_n (print.c):

  for each function i:
    if base and plen[i] is set:
      emit_print_body
    else:
      oo_wasm_u8(pay, &psz, 2);
      oo_wasm_u8(pay, &psz, 0);
      oo_wasm_u8(pay, &psz, 0x0b);

Dummy opcode quote: `02 00 0b`.

Meaning of those three bytes:

  `02`  body size 2 (LEB)
  `00`  zero local groups
  `0b`  `end`

When no print is set, plen[i] is 0 for every i.
base stays 0. Every function takes the else arm.
The body is dummy `02 00 0b`.

When some functions have a print and some do not,
the functions with plen 0 still take the else arm.
Those bodies stay dummy `02 00 0b`.

Phalanx: a name with no print keeps dummy `end`.
That contract still holds.

## 3. set_fn_print still plants WASI fd_write

set_fn_print (print.c) does not write the module.
It binds the literal, then emit_n writes the import
and the call body.

Bind path:

  1. If name_n > 0, bind_print on the last note_fn.
  2. Else store pending. note_fn later binds the last name.
  3. If emit runs first, emit_n binds pending to main
     if that name exists, else to function 0.

bind_print copies the bytes into the shared pbuf.
It adds a trailing newline if the last byte is not LF.
It sets plen[fi]. That marks a print body.

emit_n then sets base = 1 when any plen[i] is set.

When base is 1, emit_n writes import section id 2:

  module name: `wasi_snapshot_preview1` (22 bytes)
  field name:  `fd_write` (8 bytes)
  kind 0 (func), type index 1

Type section then uses ty1:

  type 0: `() -> ()`
  type 1: `(i32 i32 i32 i32) -> i32`

That is the WASI fd_write type. fd is 1 (stdout).

emit_print_body then writes:

  store iov at DATA+off and len at DATA+4
  i32.const 1, 0, 1, 8
  `call 0` (import index 0 is fd_write)
  drop
  end

Export indexes add base, so import 0 stays fd_write.

fd_write: present.

No import section when every plen[i] is 0.
Phalanx: add WASI import only when at least one
print body exists. That still holds.

## 4. emit_n does not clear ovf at entry

Function: static void oo_wasm_emit_n(unsigned int n)
print.c.

First actions:

  1. Declare pay[24000], mod[28000], and locals.
  2. Clamp n to 1 .. FN_CAP.
  3. If a pending print exists, bind it (may set ovf = 1).
  4. Scan names for main and any print body.
  5. If ovf, emit_tiny and return.

There is no `oo_wasm_ovf = 0` in those steps.
There is no `oo_wasm_ovf = 0` later in emit_n either.

The first ovf use in emit_n is a read:

  if (oo_wasm_ovf) {
    oo_wasm_emit_tiny();
    return;
  }

hv (`00 61 73 6d ...`) is copied only after this check.

ovf-clear-at-entry: no.

The only `oo_wasm_ovf = 0` in the two files is
emit_print_body. That function saves ovf, zeros it
while it builds a 64-byte scratch body, then restores
the saved value on success. That is not emit_n entry.

wasm.c leb / raw / u8 only set ovf to 1.
They never assign 0.

If ovf is set, emit_tiny writes:

  fputs("ERR\twasm\tbuf\n", stdout);

That is not a canned `\0asm` module.

## 5. Residual (no C edit this turn)

  Dummy `02 00 0b` stays for a function with no print.
  emit_tiny is a stale name for ERR text.
  FN_CAP 4096 still does not fit pay 24000 or mod 28000.
  note_fn walks until NUL or 63. It does not use name.len.
  A browser host is residual. C emit plus gcc stays the
  everyday path.

